  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 131 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThe upper level ridging over the area is moving east away from \nthe area today as an upper level trough begins to move onto the \nWest Coast. The upper trough will slowly progress eastward \ntowards the area through Friday. On Friday, models are showing a \npiece of the trough breaking off and developing an upper low over \nthe Desert Southwest, while the parent trough moves off to our \nnortheast. This upper low is then expected to move towards our \narea through the weekend. This means that we'll stay in \nsouth/southwest flow aloft through the end of the week, bringing \nmoisture into the area. Models are showing PW values around 200% \nto 350% of normal for much of the area by Thursday. Southwest \nwinds tend to favor precipitation in SW Colorado, versus our \nforecast area, but we could still see some weaker orographic lift \nwith this setup. With the influx of moisture we are expecting \nprecip chances to increase this evening in the higher terrain, \nwith showers continuing on and off through early Friday.\n\nTemperatures will be well above normal through Friday, putting \nsnow levels around 12,000 to 13,500ft. So, most of the activity\nover the next couple of days will likely be rain, with some snow \nshowers for our highest elevations. The better precip chances \nwill remain in the mountains, but CAMs are trying to bring some \nshowers onto the plains. There is potential for a Denver cyclone \ntomorrow afternoon, which could allow for weak surface \nconvergence. This, along with the potential for a weak upper \ndisturbance, could be enough with the moisture present for a few \nshowers and storms in the plains Thursday afternoon and evening. \n\nOn Friday, 700mb to 500mb winds increase over the area. Mostly clear \nskies and some QG subsidence could allow for these stronger winds\nto mix towards the surface Friday afternoon. Winds gusts around \n30 to 40mph are forecast along and south of I-76 and east of\ndowntown. A strong cold front will move into the CWA late Friday \ninto Saturday. The front will drop temperatures behind it, leaving\nnear to below normal highs for the weekend and early next week. \nThe secondary upper level trough is forecast to approach the area \nSaturday and Sunday, moving over the area on Monday. Convergence \nalong the front and upper level support from the approaching \ntrough will bring higher rain chances for the plains this weekend.\nRight now, the highest chance for heavier rainfall amounts is in \nour southeastern counties, where moisture/PWs will likely be \nhigher. \n\nPrecip chances will decrease on Monday, with drier conditions \nexpected for the start of next week. Monday will mark the start of a \ngradual warming trend, although there is potential for a front on \nWednesday to bring temperature back down a few degrees.",
